    "reasoning": [
      {
        "kind": "heading",
        "text": "Forecast: DC August 2026 original-submission ex parte renewal share"
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "The target is the District of Columbia state row for reporting period 202608 in dataset 5abea2e0-3f8e-4b49-a50d-d63d5fd9103c, using the original (O) submission at its first CMS publication. The ledger leaves the denominator implicit; CMS's state tables explicitly express ex parte renewals as a percentage of all beneficiaries due for renewal, so that convention is applied here. All historical anchors use original-period outcomes from their corresponding monthly CMS snapshots, excluding separately presented updated outcomes."
      },
      {
        "kind": "tool",
        "tool": "official.lookup",
        "call": "Fetch CMS original-period DC state tables in the July, August, and September 2025 snapshot PDFs listed in sourceContext.",
        "result": "July 2025: 21,904 beneficiaries due, 69% renewed ex parte. August 2025: 20,662 due, 71% renewed ex parte. September 2025: 18,749 due, 85% renewed ex parte. These are officially printed whole-percentage values."
      },
      {
        "kind": "tool",
        "tool": "official.lookup",
        "call": "Fetch CMS original-period DC state tables in the October, November, and December 2025 snapshot PDFs listed in sourceContext.",
        "result": "October 2025: 32,663 beneficiaries due, 89% renewed ex parte. November 2025: 24,480 due, 84% renewed ex parte. December 2025: 33,408 due, 86% renewed ex parte."
      },
      {
        "kind": "tool",
        "tool": "official.lookup",
        "call": "Fetch CMS original-period DC state tables in the January, February, and March 2026 snapshot PDFs listed in sourceContext.",
        "result": "January 2026: 18,735 beneficiaries due, 84% renewed ex parte. February 2026: 18,682 due, 84% renewed ex parte. March 2026: 18,078 due, 67% renewed ex parte."
      },
      {
        "kind": "tool",
        "tool": "official.lookup",
        "call": "Fetch CMS original-period DC state tables in the April, May, and June 2026 snapshot PDFs listed in sourceContext.",
        "result": "April 2026: 16,604 beneficiaries due, 72% renewed ex parte. May 2026: 13,652 due, 77% renewed ex parte. June 2026: 16,766 due, 70% renewed ex parte; 75% were renewed overall and 9% remained pending."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "The underlying CSV and API could not be retrieved through the web tool. HistoricalContext therefore preserves the fetched CMS snapshot percentages at their published whole-percentage precision. No raw ex parte numerator counts or one-decimal historical ratios have been invented. Resolution still requires the first-published raw O-row counts and a share rounded to one decimal."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Calendar verification gap: the official CMS release list shows June 2026 released September 25, 2026, May released August 28, and April released July 31. No official calendar or announcement confirming December 15, 2026 for the August report was found. The required resolutionDate is retained from the ledger, but this run does not satisfy the official-calendar verification requirement. Actual first publication governs resolution."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "The outside-view reference class comprises twelve consecutive DC original-period prints from July 2025 through June 2026. Their base rate is 78.1667%, while the March-June mean is 71.5%. The previous August print was 71%, supporting the recent level without establishing a reliable seasonal adjustment from a single comparison."
      },
      {
        "kind": "tool",
        "tool": "official.lookup",
        "call": "Fetch the publicly posted DHCF August 12 and August 26, 2026 eligibility presentations linked from its public eligibility-meeting index.",
        "result": "DHCF's August 12 presentation reports approximately 3,000 Medicaid cases affected by duplicated SSA income evidence following a June BENDEX enhancement; corrections were reflected in MMIS by August 3. The August 26 presentation places immigration changes on October 1, 2026 and work requirements and six-month recertification on January 1, 2027."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Level: center on the observed March-June mean. Momentum: apply no trend extrapolation because recent changes alternate direction. One-off: the corrected SSA income error creates a possible recovery mechanism, but its effect on ex parte classification is unmeasured, so apply no numerical adjustment. Policy mechanism: the cited future policy changes begin after August and receive no August adjustment. The March decline remains in dispersion rather than being discarded as an outlier."
      },
      {
        "kind": "math",
        "text": "Prior/update/interval: Use a local mean-reversion prior centered on the four March-June original-period prints: (67 + 72 + 77 + 70)/4 = 71.5%. The twelve-print historical sample has eleven successive changes [2, 14, 4, -5, 2, -2, 0, -17, 5, 5, -7] percentage points, with mean change 0.090909. Their squared deviations sum to 636.909091; sigma = 7.980658 percentage points = sqrt(636.909091/10). Momentum, one-off, seasonal, and policy adjustments are each zero. The normal-approximation one-month 80% half-width is 1.28*sigma = 10.215243 points. Scale by sqrt(2) for the June-to-August two-month horizon: half-width = 14.446535 points. Thus 71.5 +/- 14.446535 gives 57.053465 to 85.946535, rounded to final bounds 57.1% and 85.9%. These are nominal 80% bounds calibrated from the fetched, whole-percentage history."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Upside risk: corrected income records and a cohort readily matched to administrative data could lift automatic renewals; a return to October 2025's 89% would land above the interval. Downside risk: renewed data-matching errors or a cohort requiring more manual review could depress ex parte processing. Another 17-point decline, matching February-to-March's observed change, from June's 70% would produce 53% and land outside the interval below 57.1%."
      },
